\title{Strange Attractors as Algebraic Objects:\\ Inverse Limits, Transfer Matrices, and the Spectral Form of Topological Entropy}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{11 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We develop a complete algebraic theory of symbolic strange attractors modelled on finite directed graphs, and close the analytic--algebraic gap in that theory. For an arbitrary finite directed graph $E$ on a finite vertex set $V$, the space $\Lambda_E$ of infinite orbits is canonically homeomorphic to the inverse limit of the tower of finite path sets under edge deletion; the limit is compact, Hausdorff and totally disconnected, and is a Cantor set when every vertex branches. Periodic points of the shift are closed walks, hence counted by $\tr(A^n)$ for the $0/1$ transfer matrix $A$, and Cayley--Hamilton forces an integer linear recurrence of order at most $|V|$. Our main result is the Spectral Entropy Theorem: if $A$ admits a strictly positive eigenvector with eigenvalue $\lambda$, then the topological entropy is $h(E)=\log\lambda$. Consequences are uniqueness of the positive eigenvalue (the uniqueness half of Perron--Frobenius, by a purely dynamical route), the bounds $1\le\lambda\le|V|$, and the arithmetic constraint that $e^{h(E)}$ is an \emph{algebraic integer}. A Collatz--Wielandt variational construction supplies a positive eigenvector for every primitive graph, making all spectral statements unconditional and yielding simplicity, spectral dominance and positivity of entropy. Finally, for primitive graphs the closed-walk counts grow at exactly the entropy rate, so entropy and the Perron value are topological conjugacy invariants. Applied to the Lorenz template and a pruned variant we obtain $h=\log 2$ and $h=\log\varphi$, and a purely algebraic proof that the two attractors are not conjugate.
\end{abstract}

