Since $\varphi<2$ the entropies differ, and independently $\tr(A_\Lor^2)=4\neq3=\tr(A_\pr^2)$: two invariants, one analytic and one combinatorial, both separate the attractors. Deleting a single edge from a two-vertex graph turns a rational entropy exponent into a quadratic irrational.

\section{Algorithms}

\textbf{(A) Path and closed-walk counting.} Repeated squaring computes $A^n$ in $O(|V|^\omega\log n)$ ring operations; then $|P_n|=\sum_{i,j}(A^n)_{ij}$ and $|\Per_n|=\tr(A^n)$.

\textbf{(B) Characteristic polynomial.} Faddeev--LeVerrier: $M_0=I$, $c_{d-k}=-\tfrac1k\tr(AM_{k-1})$, $M_k=AM_{k-1}+c_{d-k}I$, in $O(|V|^4)$ operations. Its coefficients are the recurrence of Theorem~\ref{thm:ch}, allowing $O(|V|)$-per-step extension of the orbit-count sequence.

\textbf{(C) Perron value.} Power iteration $x\leftarrow Ax/\|Ax\|_1$ converges geometrically for primitive $A$; the Collatz--Wielandt bracket $\min_i (Ax)_i/x_i\le\lambda\le\max_i (Ax)_i/x_i$ certifies enclosures at every step, hence rigorous error bars on $h=\log\lambda$.

\textbf{(D) Primitivity testing.} By Lemma~\ref{lem:prim}, test positivity of Boolean powers up to the Wielandt bound $|V|^2-2|V|+2$.

\textbf{(E) Certified entropy.} Combine (B) and (C): isolate the largest real root of $\chi_A$ by Sturm sequences or interval Newton and report $h=\log\lambda$ with an enclosure, together with the minimal polynomial of $e^h$.

\section{Applications and discussion}

Where a template or Markov partition is available, entropy stops being an estimated quantity and becomes an algebraic one: the largest root of an explicit integer polynomial. Distinguishing attractors reduces to comparing finite data --- a single trace comparison separates the two templates. In a family of templates, pruning an edge jumps the Perron value along the algebraic integers, so entropy plateaus and jumps are transitions between roots of nearby integer polynomials. In coding terms, $\log_2\lambda$ is the capacity of the constrained channel defined by the graph: $1$ bit/symbol for the unconstrained Lorenz template, $\log_2\varphi\approx0.694$ for the ``no two consecutive $\mathsf R$'' constraint.

Three features deserve emphasis. First, the structure theorem needs no hypotheses; dead-end-freeness only makes the tower nondegenerate, and primitivity only enters for mixing, existence of Perron data, and the periodic growth rate. Second, the squeeze is the entire proof of the Spectral Entropy Theorem --- a positive eigenvector has a positive minimum and a finite maximum, and constants die when divided by $n$; this is why no irreducibility is needed and why uniqueness comes for free. Third, arithmetic constrains dynamics: a dynamical invariant is confined to a countable, arithmetically structured set, making entropy in the symbolic category an object of algebraic number theory. (Lind's classical theorem characterises the entropies of shifts of finite type as logarithms of Perron numbers; the results here derive the algebraic-integrality half directly from the inverse-limit picture, with the explicit monic polynomial in hand.)

\textit{Limitations.} This is a theory of \emph{symbolic} attractors. Reducing a smooth attractor to a finite graph requires a template or Markov partition, a hypothesis rather than a theorem proved here; and ``conjugacy'' means topological conjugacy of shifts, coarser than flow equivalence.

\section{Future directions}

\textbf{Zeta functions in full.} Prove $\zeta(t)=\exp\bigl(\sum_n|\Per_n|t^n/n\bigr)=1/\det(I-tA)$ directly, then functional equations for reversible templates; for the two templates this gives $1/(1-2t)$ and $1/(1-t-t^2)$.

\textbf{Non-primitive graphs.} Irreducible components, the Frobenius period, and cyclic decompositions should give entropy as a maximum over components --- the symbolic analogue of spectral decomposition of an Axiom A basic set.

\textbf{The realisation problem.} Which algebraic integers occur? Constructing a graph for each Perron number would turn the arithmetic constraint into an exact classification.

\textbf{Higher dimensions.} Multidimensional shifts of finite type lose most of the theory; identifying which parts of the inverse-limit machinery survive is a natural frontier.

\textbf{Other attractors.} Instantiating the theory at H\'enon and R\"ossler return graphs in hyperbolic parameter regimes would give certified entropies and algebraic separations there too.

\textbf{Quantitative bounds.} Making the eigenvector spread $C/c$ explicit via Hilbert projective-metric contraction would convert $|P_n|\asymp\lambda^n$ into effective two-sided estimates with computable constants.

\section{Conclusion}

A chaotic attractor, presented through its symbolic dynamics, is not merely amenable to algebra --- it \emph{is} an algebraic object: the inverse limit of a tower of finite path sets under edge deletion, a Cantor set carrying the shift. Its periodic orbits are the diagonal of the powers of a $0/1$ matrix and satisfy an integer linear recurrence read off from a characteristic polynomial. Its topological entropy equals the logarithm of the unique positive eigenvalue of that matrix, which exists whenever the graph is primitive, is the spectral radius, has a one-dimensional eigenspace, exceeds $1$, and is an algebraic integer. Periodic orbits grow at exactly the entropy rate, so entropy is a conjugacy invariant; and Devaney chaos follows from a single finite condition, positivity of a power of the matrix --- precisely the hypothesis of Perron--Frobenius.

